WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.194
A transaction groups changes.

00:00:05.474 --> 00:00:09.674
Here, a delete in one table and a new table, between begin and commit.

00:00:09.954 --> 00:00:13.216
The lake records both in one snapshot, or nothing at all.

00:00:14.166 --> 00:00:21.785
Change commit to rollback, and it is as if nothing happened: no files are added to the table, and no snapshot is created.

00:00:22.735 --> 00:00:26.723
In Studio's SQL Editor, every statement is its own transaction.

00:00:27.003 --> 00:00:29.769
Try to start one by hand: begin transaction.

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:36.460
Studio refuses it, and lists the statement types it accepts.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:42.729
To group changes, use a SQL client that keeps one session open, or a tool like dbt.

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:45.572
What about two writers at the same time?

00:00:45.852 --> 00:00:47.368
DuckLake is optimistic.

00:00:47.648 --> 00:00:49.078
Both work without locking.

00:00:49.358 --> 00:00:53.563
When the second one commits, it is checked against what the first one already committed.

00:00:54.513 --> 00:00:59.842
Two inserts into the same table do not conflict, and neither do changes to different tables.

00:01:00.122 --> 00:01:02.383
DuckLake simply retries the commit on top.

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:12.334
But if both delete rows from the same data file, or both change the same table's schema, the second commit fails, and that writer must try again.

00:01:13.284 --> 00:01:17.944
All of this needs a catalog that many programs can open, like PostgreSQL.

00:01:18.224 --> 00:01:24.247
Our catalog is a DuckDB file, and a DuckDB file can be opened by one program at a time.

00:01:25.197 --> 00:01:30.667
We will see that in Module 6: while Studio has the lake open, dbt cannot open it.

00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:34.393
Fine on a laptop, but a shared lake needs a shared catalog.

00:01:35.343 --> 00:01:39.022
To recap: a transaction is all or nothing, and one snapshot.

00:01:39.302 --> 00:01:43.298
In Studio's SQL Editor every statement is its own transaction.

00:01:43.578 --> 00:01:46.652
Appends do not conflict, deletes in the same file do.

00:01:46.932 --> 00:01:50.495
And a DuckDB catalog file means one program at a time.

00:01:50.775 --> 00:01:52.147
That ends Module 3.

00:01:52.427 --> 00:01:54.902
Next: performance, starting with partitioning.
